A||||6||ChadMan||Autocross tire question||||6||
Z||000000||ChadMan||04-03-2001||08:03 PM||||I am planning to compete in five or six autocross events this summer with my 88 Coupe (yes, Eric, I plan to be at the 4/22 meet!).  My primary goals are to have fun and learn to drive faster -- if I'm lucky enough to win something, that's great.<P>I am currently running two Yokohama S305's (1/2 tread left) and two Firestone Firehawk SS20's (brand new), all in 215/60R14.  I have a chance to acquire four 14" hi-tech rims with Eagle ST's already mounted, 195/70R14, tread barely legal, from another Forum member who is a hell of a great guy and will remain unnamed.<P>Do you think I'd be faster on the skinny, bald Eagles, or my fatter, treaded current tires?  And how quickly do you think I'd junk my "good" tires if I ran on them?<P>Thanks in advance, and see you at the autocross course!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000001||Oreif||04-03-2001||08:28 PM||||First, Goodyear Eagle's are only good when hot. Too hard a tire for autocross. You can get much better tires. The wheels you are buying are 6" wide so you don't want anything wider than a 215 tire due to wheel sidewall support. 205 would be better. I would also stick with a 60 or 50 series tire. Too high of a sidewall (70 or 80 series)isn't good either. (too much sidewall flex, tire rolls under during hard cornering). Finally, some tires are available shaved. I know you can get BFGoodrich comp T/A-R's (shaved comp T/A's) for race use. Also since you plan on having 2 sets of tires, You could even get a super-sticky set for autocrossing only and use your street tires the rest of the time.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||04-04-2001||10:12 AM||||Thanks for the good info, Oreif!  I will keep all that in mind if I do well this year and decide to get serious next year.<P>In the meantime, I think I'll go ahead and get the bald Eagles.  I might not be competitive on them, but I'll still learn a lot.  If my times come down as the year goes by, maybe I'll invest in some Kumho competition tires and see if I can actually beat somebody.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||04-04-2001||11:31 AM||||quite a few guys here are running NITTO NT555Rs yeah they are a DOT drag tire but they seem to pull better numbers on this than on the Yokohama A032s i was running. a decent daily driver and corner carving tire is the Yokohama AVS intermediate. they have a 140 treadwear not a 60 like the A032, the 00 of the NT555s or the BFGRs. and they arent' that pricey either. I've got them on my Alfa 164L and GT Bastard has them on his 86 SE... 1.13g! theres somthing to be said for that!<BR> <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tirerack.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.tires.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tires.com</A>  <P>another thing.. a stout rear anti-sway bar.. and ditch the fron tbar.. steer with the throttle my car under any real acceleration will plow straight into the weeds.. but a bar on the rear and ditched the front.. it's really sensative on the highway but will lock the seat belt in a good corner!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||04-04-2001||05:37 PM||||All I can say is if you got the money...<BR>HOOSIERS!<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72 Race Director<BR>#47 CSP<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">   <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||04-04-2001||09:15 PM||||I agree. Hoosiers have some holding power. Wish I could afford a set.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||04-04-2001||11:50 PM||||Hey Chadma, I'm here now.  I don't know if I'd be too concerned with your tire setup right now because it takes a while to learn how your tires will grip and under what conditions.  Sometimes used tires that aren't shaved loose their stickum and become slippery.  Get some 60 grit sandpaper and rubber softener and just work that stuff in.  I'm going to be running on my yodohama s306s (I think they're just one better than the 305s if my math is correct).  I ran a509s last year, but, of course, the tread runs out in like 2000 miles so I don't have them still.  They stuck like crazy glue on a cat's ass.  I guess the intermediates are of the same compound.  I'm trying to convince dad to use those tires on his Cobra if we can still get them.  I might have to retire the Fiero or find another driver once we get that thing running  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
